How the Internet Works:
// The internet in a nutshell
// Your browser (client) sends a request
// DNS translates domain -> IP address
// TCP establishes a connection
// Server processes and sends back a response
// Your browser renders the HTML

// This entire flow happens in milliseconds!

What is HTTP:
// HTTP Request/Response cycle
// Request:
GET /api/users HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
Authorization: Bearer <token>
Accept: application/json

// Response:
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
{
  "users": [
    {"id": 1, "name": "Alice"}
  ]
}

DNS & How It Works:
// DNS resolution steps:
// 1. Browser checks local DNS cache
// 2. Checks OS hosts file
// 3. Queries recursive DNS resolver (ISP)
// 4. Resolver queries root nameserver
// 5. Queries TLD nameserver (.com)
// 6. Queries authoritative nameserver
// 7. Returns IP address to browser

// Result: example.com -> 93.184.216.34

What is Hosting:
// Hosting types comparison:
// Shared:   Cheap, limited resources, "noisy neighbors"
// VPS:      Isolated virtual machine, root access
// Dedicated: Full physical server, maximum performance
// Cloud:    Auto-scaling, pay-as-you-go (AWS, GCP, Azure)
// Serverless: Just deploy code, no server management

Browsers & How They Work:
// Browser rendering pipeline:
// 1. Parse HTML -> DOM Tree
// 2. Parse CSS -> CSSOM Tree
// 3. Combine -> Render Tree
// 4. Layout -> Box model calculations
// 5. Paint -> Pixel rasterization
// 6. Composite -> Final screen output

// JavaScript can block parsing!
// <script defer> or async for performance

What is a Domain Name:
// Domain structure:
// blog.example.co.uk
//  |     |     |   |
//  |     |     |   TLD (.uk)
//  |     |     SLD (.co)
//  |     Domain (example)
